{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1766 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c3b283ba05e30bc56d2ad2883796af74d4d89d72070aee3f678ae8872f77edf9" :edb/db-before-t 1765 :edb/db-after-t 1766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535078 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:22:43.282Z" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119486 1100 "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/assets/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119486 1100 "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$ to $t = 2\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/0c0c2dffe8d85c38943e919e899e3373.png)" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119486 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin 2t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 3\\cos t, 4\\cos 2t\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert f^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(3\\cos t)^{2} + (4\\cos 2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{a}^{b}\\Vert f^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119486 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119487 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/bfcb501a54f7ab19db15a4c4726f7f63.png)" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119487 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/assets/29c97e5963d451cc389944ef113764b2d15998448e773a02bb98c062207c2860.png)" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119487 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(3 - 2\\sin 2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - 2\\cos 2t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle  - 4\\cos 2t, 4\\sin 2t\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert g^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(-4\\cos 2t)^{2} + (4\\sin 2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} 2t + 16\\sin^{2} 2t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{a}^{b}\\Vert g^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4dt \\\\\n&= 4t \\mid _{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\\n&= 4(\\pi  - \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8\\pi }{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119487 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119599 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/assets/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119599 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/3fca46c2b71edab0ded0e3d361c48c4f.png)" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119599 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\langle \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle \\cos t,-\\sin t, 1\\rangle  \\\\\n\\Vert r^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert  &= \\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{0}^{2\\pi }\\Vert r^{\\prime }(t)\\Vert dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}t \\mid _{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}(2\\pi  - 0) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\\pi \n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186119599 1145 "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186119667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330036 13194139535078 true] [17592186119667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330043 13194139535078 true] [17592186119667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330057 13194139535078 true] [17592186119668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330050 13194139535078 true] [17592186119668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330092 13194139535078 true] [17592186119668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330099 13194139535078 true] [17592186119668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330106 13194139535078 true] [17592186119781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330064 13194139535078 true] [17592186119781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330071 13194139535078 true] [17592186119781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330078 13194139535078 true] [17592186119781 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330085 13194139535078 true] [17592186120205 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/assets/d162245ac19c3ef9ae63d602fb4e188ddfd241a1a604304f6c272c3902ce1e93.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120205 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/q-33728.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g} ( t ) = \\langle  \\sin   2 t , \\, \\cos   3 t \\rangle$ from $t = 0$ to $t = \\pi $?" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120205 1145 "Differentiate to get $\\mathbf g'(t)=\\langle2\\cos(2t),-3\\sin(3t)\\rangle$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{4\\cos^2(2t)+9\\sin^2(3t)}$. Thus $$L=\\int_0^\\pi\\sqrt{4\\cos^2(2t)+9\\sin^2(3t)}\\,dt.$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120205 1145 "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120208 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f} ( t ) = \\langle \\cos   t + t \\sin   t , \\, \\sin   t - t \\cos   t , \\, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} {t}^{2} \\rangle$\n\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = 3$." 13194139535078 false] [17592186120208 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120208 1145 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120208 1145 "The product rule gives $\\mathbf f'(t)=\\langle t\\cos t,t\\sin t,\\sqrt3t\\rangle$. For $1\\le t\\le3$, its magnitude is $\\sqrt{t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t+3)}=2t$. Hence $$L=\\int_1^3 2t\\,dt=[t^2]_1^3=9-1=8.$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535078 true] [17592186120214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535078 false] [17592186120214 1100 "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)={e}^{t}(\\,i+2\\,j+t\\,k)$ from  $t=1$ to $t=2?$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120214 1100 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{f} ( t ) = {e}^{t} ( \\, \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + t \\, \\mathbf{k} )$ from $t = 1$ to $t = 2$?" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120214 1145 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120214 1145 "Write $\\mathbf f(t)=\\langle e^t,2e^t,te^t\\rangle$. The product rule gives $\\mathbf f'(t)=e^t\\langle1,2,t+1\\rangle$, so $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|=e^t\\sqrt{1+4+(t+1)^2}$. Therefore $$L=\\int_1^2e^t\\sqrt{t^2+2t+6}\\,dt.$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535078 true] [17592186120454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535078 false] [17592186120454 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/assets/b614e2c3e6abf0d7ddb8399aa7ea78b7a0e618a965ee7b5481edf08310a45031.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=2?$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120454 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1837/Source/Images/q-77065.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{g} ( t ) = \\langle  {t}^{3} - 3 t , \\, 3 {t}^{2} \\rangle$ from $t = 1$ to $t = 2$?" 13194139535078 false] [17592186120454 1145 "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186120454 1145 "The velocity is $\\mathbf g'(t)=\\langle3t^2-3,6t\\rangle$. Its speed simplifies to $$\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|=3\\sqrt{(t^2-1)^2+4t^2}=3\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^2}=3(t^2+1).$$ Hence $$L=\\int_1^2 3(t^2+1)\\,dt=[t^3+3t]_1^2=14-4=10.$$" 13194139535078 false] [17592186330036 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330037 13194139535078 true] [17592186330036 1089 "q-127419" 13194139535078 true] [17592186330036 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535078 true] [17592186330036 1100 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ from $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186330036 1113 #uuid "20f7dd35-57f3-5479-bd92-ca0b991a0b49" 13194139535078 true] [17592186330036 1145 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330038 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330039 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330040 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330041 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330042 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1041 "selection" 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330039 13194139535078 true] [17592186330037 1110 #uui